---
/**
 * Deploying mcpd.
 *
 * The manifests on this page are not transcribed. They are read from
 * `deploy/` at build time, so the file a reader copies is the file that is in
 * the repository. A drifted example is worse than no example.
 */
import fs from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Unreleased from '@/components/Unreleased.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

const ROOT = path.resolve(process.cwd(), '..')
const read = (rel: string) => fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, rel), 'utf8').trimEnd()

const docker = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'docker-compose.yml',
    shiki: 'yaml',
    code: read('deploy/docker/docker-compose.yml'),
  },
  {
    lang: 'kotlin' as const,
    label: '.env.example',
    shiki: 'bash',
    code: read('deploy/docker/.env.example'),
  },
]

const kubernetes = [
  { lang: 'typescript' as const, label: 'deployment.yaml', shiki: 'yaml', code: read('deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml') },
  { lang: 'python' as const, label: 'service.yaml', shiki: 'yaml', code: read('deploy/k8s/service.yaml') },
  { lang: 'go' as const, label: 'pvc.yaml', shiki: 'yaml', code: read('deploy/k8s/pvc.yaml') },
  { lang: 'rust' as const, label: 'secret.yaml.example', shiki: 'yaml', code: read('deploy/k8s/secret.yaml.example') },
]

const chartValues = [
  { lang: 'curl' as const, label: 'values.yaml', shiki: 'yaml', code: read('deploy/helm/mcpd/values.yaml') },
]
---

<Doc
  title="Docker and Kubernetes"
  description="Run mcpd as a container or on Kubernetes: a compose file, manifests with real probes, a Helm chart, and the two keys to set before the port is reachable by anything else."
  lede="One process, one volume, one file to back up. The longest section on this page is the one about what to set before anyone but you can reach it."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>One container</h2>
  <Unreleased class="not-prose my-6" />
  <p>
    mcpd is a single process with its storage compiled in. There is no database
    to run beside it, no sidecar, and no static-file server: ingest, the query
    API and the interface are all served from one port.
  </p>

  <Command
    command="docker run -p 4317:4317 -v mcpd-data:/data -e MCPD_DATA=/data/mcpd.db ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd:0.1.0"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Print the configuration and exit', command: 'docker run --rm ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd:0.1.0 config' },
      { label: 'Keep nothing', command: 'docker run -p 4317:4317 -e MCPD_DATA=:memory: ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd:0.1.0' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The volume is the part worth reading twice. Without it the SQLite file lives
    in the container's writable layer, and the traces go when the container
    does. <code>MCPD_DATA</code> is set explicitly for the same reason: if the
    path the daemon writes and the path you mounted ever drift apart, mcpd keeps
    working and the volume stays empty, which is the kind of mistake you find
    out about on the day you need the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pin the tag. <code>latest</code> makes a restart into an upgrade, at a time
    nobody chose. Each tag is built for <code>linux/amd64</code> and
    <code>linux/arm64</code>, and runs as uid 10001, which owns
    <code>/data</code> in the image.
  </p>

  <h2>The port, and what answers on it</h2>
  <p>
    Everything is on 4317. That is what makes the deployment one container, and
    it is also what you need to know when you write an ingress rule or a network
    policy, because the paths do not all carry the same risk.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Path</th>
        <th>What it is</th>
        <th>Key required</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code>, <code>/v1/metrics</code></td>
        <td>OTLP ingest, for any OpenTelemetry SDK</td>
        <td>Ingest</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/ingest</code></td>
        <td>Native ingest, the full model in one request</td>
        <td>Ingest</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/api/…</code></td>
        <td>The query API: traces, spans, logs, scores, analytics</td>
        <td>Read</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/</code></td>
        <td>The operator interface, embedded in the binary</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/healthz</code>, <code>/readyz</code>, <code>/metrics</code></td>
        <td>Liveness, readiness, and mcpd's own Prometheus counters</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    An ingest request is capped at 32 MiB. Prompts are large, so that limit is
    generous on purpose, but a proxy in front of mcpd usually has a smaller one:
    a reverse proxy that refuses a 3 MiB batch is the most common reason a trace
    never arrives.
  </p>

  <h2>Compose</h2>
  <p>
    Two decisions in this file are worth explaining, because both look like
    omissions. The port is published on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, so nothing
    outside the host can reach it while you are still setting keys. And the file
    has no healthcheck because the image has one: it requests
    <code>/healthz</code> every 30 seconds with the busybox <code>wget</code> in
    the image, on the port in <code>MCPD_LISTEN</code>, and
    <code>docker compose ps</code> shows the result. Docker does not restart a
    container for being unhealthy; the restart policy acts when the process
    exits.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={docker} title="deploy/docker" />

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    command="docker compose -f deploy/docker/docker-compose.yml up -d"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Logs', command: 'docker compose -f deploy/docker/docker-compose.yml logs -f mcpd' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The <code>:?</code> in each key line is not decoration. Compose refuses to
    start the service when the variable is unset, which turns a forgotten key
    from a silent open port into an error message. The two run keys have no
    value at all in the file, so Compose passes them only when
    <code>.env</code> sets them: an empty value would reach the daemon as one
    empty key rather than none.
  </p>

  <h2>Kubernetes</h2>
  <p>
    Four objects: a Deployment, a Service, a PersistentVolumeClaim, and a Secret
    you create yourself. The Deployment runs one replica with the
    <code>Recreate</code> strategy, and that is the one line in these manifests
    you should not change. mcpd stores everything in a single SQLite file, one
    process writes it, and a rolling update would start a second pod on the same
    volume while the first still holds it. That is not a scaling story, it is a
    corruption story.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Secret is not one of the files <code>kubectl apply</code> reads.
    <code>secret.yaml.example</code> shows its shape, and the extension keeps it
    out of the apply, because a Secret applied as written would make its
    placeholder strings your keys. Create it from the command line instead,
    with keys generated on the spot. The pod runs as uid 10001 with a read-only
    root filesystem, no capabilities and the runtime's default seccomp profile,
    which is what the Pod Security <code>restricted</code> level asks for.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={kubernetes} title="deploy/k8s" />

  <pre><code class="language-bash">{`export INGEST_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) READ_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) ADMIN_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
kubectl create namespace mcpd
kubectl -n mcpd create secret generic mcpd-keys \\
  --from-literal=ingest-keys=$INGEST_KEY \\
  --from-literal=read-keys=$READ_KEY \\
  --from-literal=admin-keys=$ADMIN_KEY \\
  --from-literal=redact-key=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
kubectl -n mcpd apply -f deploy/k8s/`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Keep the three keys where your team keeps secrets: the ingest key goes to
    every agent, the read key to whoever reads the interface, and the admin key
    to whoever may delete data. Add <code>execute-keys</code> and
    <code>worker-keys</code> the same way once a worker runs outside the
    cluster; until then the ingest keys are accepted for both.
  </p>

  <pre class="my-6"><code>{`export EXECUTE_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) WORKER_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
kubectl -n mcpd patch secret mcpd-keys --type merge \\
  -p '{"stringData":{"execute-keys":"'$EXECUTE_KEY'","worker-keys":"'$WORKER_KEY'"}}'
kubectl -n mcpd rollout restart deploy/mcpd`}</code></pre>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    command="kubectl -n mcpd port-forward svc/mcpd 4317:4317"
    primaryLabel="Reach it from your machine"
  />

  <p>
    Agents inside the cluster point at the Service. The SDKs read both of these
    from the environment, so instrumented code needs no change:
  </p>

  <Command command="MCPD_ENDPOINT=http://mcpd.mcpd.svc:4317 MCPD_API_KEY=$INGEST_KEY" />

  <h3>The probes</h3>
  <p>
    The two endpoints do deliberately different things, and swapping them causes
    a specific, ugly failure.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>/healthz</code> never touches storage. It answers from process state
      alone. A liveness probe that queried the database would restart a healthy
      daemon every time a large query made it slow, and restarting is exactly
      what you do not want while a write-ahead log is being flushed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>/readyz</code> pings the store and returns 503 with
      <code>"status": "degraded"</code> and a line saying what is wrong when it
      cannot. That keeps traffic away from an instance that cannot serve it,
      without killing it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The startup probe is there because migrations run at start. They are
    idempotent and usually instant, but on a large database they can take longer
    than the liveness probe would tolerate, and a pod that gets killed
    mid-migration on every attempt never starts at all.
  </p>

  <h3>What to give it</h3>
  <p>
    These numbers come from what the process actually does, not from a
    benchmark, and no benchmark is claimed.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Resource</th>
        <th>Value</th>
        <th>Why</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>CPU request</td>
        <td><code>100m</code></td>
        <td>Idle costs close to nothing. The request exists so the scheduler does not stack it under something noisy.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>CPU limit</td>
        <td>none</td>
        <td>Ingest arrives in bursts. Throttling the writer during a burst is how a client queue fills and spans get dropped.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Memory request</td>
        <td><code>128Mi</code></td>
        <td>A static binary, five SQLite connections and their page caches.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Memory limit</td>
        <td><code>512Mi</code></td>
        <td>Headroom for concurrent ingest. One request can hold 32 MiB of prompt text, so memory tracks how many large batches arrive at once.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Volume</td>
        <td><code>20Gi</code>, ReadWriteOnce</td>
        <td>Sized from your own payloads. Text dominates, and the search index is not an external-content one, so indexed prompts and completions are stored a second time.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Keep the volume on a local block device. SQLite's locking is only correct
    there: on NFS or another shared filesystem the locks are advisory at best,
    and the failure does not show up as an error, it shows up as a corrupt
    database weeks later.
  </p>

  <h2>The Helm chart</h2>
  <p>
    The chart renders the same four objects with values in front of them, plus
    six that are off until a value turns them on: an Ingress, a NetworkPolicy,
    a PodDisruptionBudget, a ServiceAccount, a ServiceMonitor, and a ConfigMap
    or Secret for the config file. Each release pushes it to the same registry
    as the image. Generate the keys first and keep them where your team keeps
    secrets, because the install will not print them back to you:
  </p>

  <Command
    command="helm install mcpd oci://ghcr.io/mcpd/charts/mcpd -n mcpd --create-namespace --set keys.ingest=$INGEST_KEY --set keys.read=$READ_KEY"
    primaryLabel="Install from the registry"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Generate the keys', command: 'export INGEST_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32) READ_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)' },
      { label: 'From a clone', command: 'helm install mcpd ./deploy/helm/mcpd -n mcpd --create-namespace -f values.yaml' },
      { label: 'Upgrade', command: 'helm upgrade mcpd oci://ghcr.io/mcpd/charts/mcpd -n mcpd --reset-then-reuse-values' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The install fails when <code>keys.ingest</code> or <code>keys.read</code> is
    unset, and says why. A chart that let you discover an open ingest endpoint
    later would not be doing its job. If you manage the Secret yourself, set
    <code>keys.existingSecret</code> instead and the chart creates none.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pod template carries a checksum of the Secret, so rotating a key and
    running <code>helm upgrade</code> restarts the pod. Without that, the new
    key sits in the cluster and the daemon keeps enforcing the old one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Monitors, SLOs, the judge, per-project keys and the run store's timings are
    read from the daemon's config file, not from flags. Write that JSON object
    under <code>configFile.content</code> and the chart mounts it read-only and
    points <code>MCPD_CONFIG_FILE</code> at it. Per-project keys, the judge's
    <code>api_key</code> and forward headers are credentials, so the chart
    refuses to put content carrying any of them in a ConfigMap: set
    <code>configFile.kind</code> to <code>Secret</code>, or keep the file in a
    Secret you manage and name it in <code>configFile.existingSecret</code>.
    <code>extraEnv</code>, <code>extraVolumes</code> and
    <code>extraVolumeMounts</code> cover anything else, such as a private CA
    bundle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every combination the chart cannot serve is refused at install time with a
    message that names the value to change, never left to crash-loop after it.
    The chart's README lists each refusal.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={chartValues} title="deploy/helm/mcpd" />

  <h2>Before anything else can reach it</h2>
  <p>
    Ingest is open by default. That is a deliberate choice for the first five
    minutes on a laptop, where a credential before the first trace would be
    friction with no benefit, and it is the wrong default the moment the port is
    reachable by anything else. So the daemon says so, every time it starts on a
    non-loopback address without keys:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="what an unprotected start looks like"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'docker run -p 4317:4317 -v mcpd-data:/data ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd:0.1.0' },
      { out: 'mcpd 0.1.0 listening on http://0.0.0.0:4317', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'WARN listening on 0.0.0.0:4317 with open ingest. Anyone who can', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '     reach this port can write to it. Set --ingest-key (or', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '     MCPD_INGEST_KEYS) before exposing it.', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'WARN listening on 0.0.0.0:4317 with an open read API. Stored', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '     prompts and completions are readable by anyone who can reach', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '     this port. Set --read-key to require a key.', tone: 'warn' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    mcpd warns rather than refusing to start, because refusing would break the
    path that makes it worth running at all. The warning is the contract: if you
    see it in a deployment log, that deployment is open.
  </p>

  <h3>Six keys, six scopes</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--ingest-key</code> permits writing signals. <code>--read-key</code>
    permits reading them. <code>--write-key</code> permits changing stored data:
    scores, feedback, datasets, experiments and acknowledgements.
    <code>--execute-key</code> permits starting, cancelling and signalling runs.
    <code>--worker-key</code> permits claiming steps and reporting what happened
    to them, and is the key that lives in the process running your step bodies.
    <code>--admin-key</code> permits deleting a trace and reading the audit log.
    An unset list accepts a wider one in its place: write accepts the ingest
    keys, admin accepts the read keys, and execute and worker accept the write
    keys. A deployment configured before a scope existed keeps working.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first split is the one that decides your blast radius, because the
    populations are different sizes: every agent process in your fleet needs the
    ingest key, and a much smaller number of people need any of the others.
    Handing your fleet one key that also reads would put a credential for every
    stored prompt into every container that emits a span, including the ones
    running third-party code.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also why capturing a run into a dataset moved to the write scope. It
    reads the run's input and output on the way through, so authorising it with
    the key your fleet carries handed every one of those containers a way to copy
    any stored prompt out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each flag takes a comma-separated list and each has an environment variable:
    <code>MCPD_INGEST_KEYS</code>, <code>MCPD_READ_KEYS</code>,
    <code>MCPD_WRITE_KEYS</code> and <code>MCPD_ADMIN_KEYS</code>. Use more than
    one: a key per team or per fleet means a leaked key is revoked by removing it
    from the list, not by re-deploying everything that emits a span. A key is
    presented as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> or in the
    <code>X-Mcpd-Key</code> header, and is compared in constant time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The asymmetry to know about is what happens when you leave the two newer
    flags unset. An unset write list accepts the ingest keys and an unset admin
    list accepts the read keys, so an upgrade never revokes a key that was
    working. Until you set <code>--admin-key</code>, a read key is a key to read
    and to delete, not a strictly read-only one, and the daemon says so at
    startup. Give it out accordingly, or set the flag.
  </p>

  <h3>Who read what</h3>
  <p>
    The handlers that hand payloads back record the access: the trace detail, the
    span and log searches, feedback, dataset items, capture, and delete. A row
    carries the time, a short digest of the key that acted, the action, the
    project, the resource and whether authorisation allowed it. The key itself is
    never written down, so the log is not a second place your credentials live.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read it with <code>GET /v1/api/audit</code>, which takes the admin scope.
    Two properties are worth knowing before you rely on it. It is not trimmed by
    <code>--retention-days</code>, because a log that deleted itself on the same
    schedule as the data it describes could not say who read the data that has
    since gone. And writing a row never fails the read it describes: a row that
    cannot be written is counted as
    <code>mcpd_audit_write_failures_total</code> on <code>/metrics</code>, which
    is the counter to alert on.
  </p>

  <h3>What a key does not cover</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The interface.</strong> The markup at <code>/</code> is served to
      anyone who can reach the port. The data behind it is not: every request it
      makes goes through the query API, which enforces the read key. There is no
      login screen in the daemon, so if people rather than services need access,
      put it behind the identity proxy you already run.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The probes and <code>/metrics</code>.</strong> Unauthenticated, on
      purpose, because a kubelet and a Prometheus scraper cannot carry a key.
      They expose counters and uptime, never payloads.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transport.</strong> mcpd speaks plain HTTP and does not terminate
      TLS. Terminate it at your ingress or in your mesh. Prompts and completions
      cross that link in full.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hashes in redacted payloads.</strong> Redaction is on unless you
      pass <code>--no-redact</code>, and where it hashes an identifier the digest
      is only as private as its salt. Set <code>--redact-key</code>, or short
      identifiers can be recovered by guessing. See
      <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Check before you expose it</h3>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd config</code> prints the configuration an invocation would use
    and exits. It is the cheapest way to find out that the environment variable
    you set in the wrong place did nothing:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="pre-flight"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'docker compose -f deploy/docker/docker-compose.yml run --rm mcpd config' },
      { out: 'listen          0.0.0.0:4317' },
      { out: 'storage         sqlite at /data/mcpd.db' },
      { out: 'config file     none' },
      { out: 'log format      json' },
      { out: 'project         default' },
      { out: 'projects        none' },
      { out: 'retention       30 days' },
      { out: 'ingest auth     key required', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'read auth       key required', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'write auth      key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'admin auth      key required (read keys)', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'execute auth    key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'worker auth     key required (ingest keys)', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'signup          off' },
      { out: 'runs            sqlite at /data/mcpd.runs.db' },
      { out: 'run settings    lease_ttl_ms 30000, reclaim_window_ms 30000, stall_window_ms 300000, idempotency_window_ms 86400000, park_ttl_ms 86400000, max_depth 8, max_step_repeats 16' },
      { out: 'identity        off' },
      { out: 'client ip       peer address' },
      { out: 'sampling        keep everything' },
      { out: 'quota           unlimited' },
      { out: 'redaction       on' },
      { out: 'redact key      set', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'cors            same-origin only' },
      { out: 'timeout         30s per request' },
      { out: 'limits          32 MiB per request, 10000 signals per batch' },
      { out: 'derived         duration and token metrics' },
      { out: 'aspects         off' },
      { out: 'monitors        none' },
      { out: 'objectives      none' },
      { out: 'judge           off' },
      { out: 'forwarding      off' },
      { out: 'relay           off: no --upstream' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Six lines matter here. If any auth line reads <code>open</code>, that scope
    is open to whoever can reach the port. A line in brackets names the wider
    list that scope is inheriting from: the invocation above sets no
    <code>--admin-key</code>, so its read keys delete as well as read. It sets no
    <code>--execute-key</code> or <code>--worker-key</code> either, so its ingest
    keys also start runs and claim steps, and mcpd warns about that when it
    starts. Every flag
    this prints is documented on
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <p>
    The backup is one file. Everything mcpd stores, including the search index,
    is in the file at <code>MCPD_DATA</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Copying that file while the daemon runs is the one thing not to do. mcpd
    runs SQLite in WAL mode, so recent writes live in a <code>-wal</code> file
    beside it, and a copy of the database alone is a copy that is missing them.
    Take a consistent copy instead, from any image that has the
    <code>sqlite3</code> tool and mounts the same volume:
  </p>

  <Command
    command={`sqlite3 /data/mcpd.db ".backup '/backup/mcpd-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M).db'"`}
    primaryLabel="Online backup"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'Compacted copy', command: 'sqlite3 /data/mcpd.db "VACUUM INTO \'/backup/mcpd.db\'"' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Both read a consistent snapshot while writes continue. A volume snapshot
    taken by your CSI driver works too, if the driver's snapshot is atomic; a
    <code>tar</code> of a live directory is not. Restoring is stopping the
    daemon, putting the file back at <code>MCPD_DATA</code>, deleting the
    <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code> files beside it, and starting the
    daemon again; <a href="/docs/operations" class="link">Running it</a> walks
    through it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deleted rows free pages inside the file, they do not shrink it, so a
    database that has aged out a lot of data stays the size it was. Run
    <code>VACUUM</code> when you want the disk back.
  </p>

  <h2>Upgrades</h2>
  <p>
    Change the tag and restart. Migrations run at start and are safe to run on
    every start, so no step happens outside the process. Take the backup first
    anyway: a migration is the one moment a schema changes under your data.
  </p>
  <p>
    The restart is a short outage, not a rolling one, for the same reason there
    is one replica. Agents survive it: the SDKs queue in memory and retry, and
    the queue is bounded, so a restart that takes seconds costs nothing and one
    that takes an hour drops the oldest spans and says so in the client's log.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd handles SIGTERM and shuts down gracefully, which is what Kubernetes
    sends. <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> has to outlast the longest
    request, or the kubelet kills the process mid-write. A request may run for
    the 30-second request timeout, so the manifests and the chart give the pod
    45 seconds, and the compose file gives the container the same. Raise both
    together.
  </p>

  <h2>Where one SQLite file stops, and what to do about it</h2>
  <p>
    The embedded file is the right default and it has two hard limits. Both
    belong to SQLite, not to mcpd.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One writer.</strong> Reads run on their own connections and never
      block the writer, which is what WAL mode is for, but one process owns the
      file. You scale that instance up, not out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One volume, one availability zone.</strong> A ReadWriteOnce claim
      pins the pod. There is no failover to another node with the data intact.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two settings usually buy back more than a new backend would.
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a> keeps a fraction of
    traces and keeps each kept trace whole, deciding by hashing the trace id so
    every instance in a fleet agrees. Retention caps how far back the file goes.
    Running one instance per team, each with its own volume, also works and
    keeps a noisy team from filling everyone's disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    When those run out, point mcpd at a database. Set
    <code>externalStore.type</code> to <code>postgres</code> or
    <code>clickhouse</code> in the chart, with the connection string in
    <code>externalStore.postgresUrl</code> or
    <code>externalStore.clickhouseUrl</code>. The string is held in the chart's
    Secret, not in the pod spec. To keep it out of your values altogether, put
    it in a Secret you manage under <code>postgres-url</code> or
    <code>clickhouse-url</code>, name that Secret in
    <code>keys.existingSecret</code>, and set the type all the same: the type,
    not the URL, selects the backend.
  </p>
  <p>
    That moves the signals and leaves one file on disk: the run store, which is
    embedded SQLite whatever the signal store is. With
    <code>runs.enabled</code> true, the default, the chart keeps it on the
    volume and runs one replica. To run several pods, turn the run surface off.
    The chart then runs <code>replicaCount</code> pods, switches from
    <code>Recreate</code> to a rolling update, and drops the
    PersistentVolumeClaim, because nothing local is left to fight over, and the
    daemon answers the run endpoints with a 503 that says so. The chart refuses
    a <code>replicaCount</code> above 1 while runs are on, rather than starting
    pods that each hold a run store of their own.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-bash">{`helm install mcpd ./deploy/helm/mcpd \\
  --set keys.ingest=$INGEST_KEY \\
  --set keys.read=$READ_KEY \\
  --set externalStore.type=postgres \\
  --set externalStore.postgresUrl=postgres://mcpd:$PGPASSWORD@db:5432/mcpd \\
  --set runs.enabled=false \\
  --set replicaCount=3`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Reach for Postgres first: it lifts both limits on a database most teams
    already run, back up and monitor. Migration takes an advisory lock and is
    skipped when the schema is current, so several replicas starting together is
    safe. Reach for ClickHouse when retention is measured in billions of spans.
    A URL for a backend the type does not name is refused rather than ignored.
  </p>
  <p>
    One thing does not change with the backend.
    <strong>Retention covers the projects you wrote down.</strong> The hourly
    pass deletes signals older than <code>--retention-days</code> in the project
    named by <code>--project</code> and in every project in the
    <code>projects</code> block, each on its own <code>retention_days</code>
    where it sets one. Traces sent under a project name that appears in neither
    place have no window and are never trimmed, so watch the volume if your
    agents set their own.
  </p>

